<h1>OpenLogi Flatpak repository</h1>
<p>
  The Flatpak build of <a href="https://github.com/AprilNEA/OpenLogi">OpenLogi</a>,
  currently <strong>@VERSION@</strong>.
</p>
<p>
  It exists because the other Linux packages ship without a repository, so they
  cannot update themselves. This one can: install once, and
  <code>flatpak update</code> keeps it current.
</p>
<h2>Install</h2>
<pre><code>flatpak remote-add --if-not-exists --user openlogi @BASE@/openlogi.flatpakrepo
flatpak install --user openlogi org.openlogi.OpenLogi</code></pre>
<p>Updates then arrive with <code>flatpak update</code>, or automatically via GNOME Software / KDE Discover.</p>
<h2>One-time host setup</h2>
<p>
  A Flatpak cannot write to <code>/etc</code>, so OpenLogi's udev rules must be
  installed on the host. <strong>Until you do this, no devices are detected.</strong>
  The rules ship inside the app:
</p>
<pre><code>flatpak run --command=cat org.openlogi.OpenLogi \
  /app/share/openlogi/udev/70-openlogi.rules \
  | sudo tee /etc/udev/rules.d/70-openlogi.rules

echo uinput | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/openlogi.conf
sudo modprobe uinput

sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
sudo udevadm trigger</code></pre>
<p>
  The <code>uinput</code> lines matter on a machine that has never loaded that
  module: the rule creates the device node, but its ACL is only granted in
  response to a device event, and with no module loaded there is no device for
  <code>udevadm trigger</code> to act on.
</p>
<p>
  Then quit OpenLogi completely (<code>flatpak kill org.openlogi.OpenLogi</code>)
  and start it again. The background agent outlives the window and installs its
  input hook once at startup, so a permission granted while it runs does not
  reach it.
</p>
<h2>Problems?</h2>
<p>
  Report anything that goes wrong on the
  <a href="https://github.com/AprilNEA/OpenLogi/issues">issue tracker</a>.
</p>
